   const foilHelp=el('details',{id:'wxFoilAlgorithm'},el('summary',{text:'Independent 2D airfoil solver · how to use it'}),el('div',{html:`
     <p>Choose Aero results → 2D airfoil, or select a rib and press Analyze this section (2D). Choose a rib/station, enter y in mm, or enter η = y / projected semi-span (0 root, 1 tip). For a 450 mm semi-wing, η = 0.5 means y = 225 mm. Ribs are structural parts; they do not create the airfoil or change its 2D pressure. An exact zero-chord tip has no airfoil section; choose an inboard station.</p>
-    <p>Enter angle of attack relative to that section's own chord and panels per side, then press Solve 2D airfoil. This uses the local profile, not the 3D pressure solution, and needs no wing solve. Enter α directly; wing incidence is not silently added. Sweep, dihedral, finite-span downwash and the rounded aerodynamic tip are not part of the isolated 2D problem. For pressure from the finite wing's 3D solution instead, choose Sectional Cp vs x/c and its solved strip. The two results need not agree.</p>
+    <p><b>Why two stations can show the same 2D Cp.</b> The local profile is extracted from the wing and normalized to a unit chord, then solved again as an isolated airfoil. At the same normalized profile, entered α and panel count, this inviscid 2D problem is identical at the root and near the tip: changing chord, rib or rod position does not create wing downwash in a 2D calculation. Different local profiles or entered α do change the answer. Real Reynolds-number effects are excluded.</p>
+    <p><b>To include the finite wing:</b> solve Aerodynamics, then choose <b>3D wing · sectional Cp</b> or press <b>3D Cp near this station</b>. That view reads the closest actually solved 3D strip and displays its y coordinate; it does not substitute or interpolate an isolated 2D result. Spanwise interaction, downwash and tip geometry can make its Cp vary with y. See <a href="https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/downwash-effects-on-lift/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA: finite-wing downwash</a>.</p>
+    <p>Enter angle of attack relative to that section's own chord and panels per side, then press Solve 2D airfoil. This uses the local profile, not the 3D pressure solution, and needs no wing solve. Enter α directly; wing incidence is not silently added. Sweep, dihedral, finite-span downwash and the rounded aerodynamic tip are not part of the isolated 2D problem. For pressure from the finite wing's 3D solution instead, choose 3D wing · sectional Cp vs x/c and its solved strip. The two results need not agree.</p>
     <p>The contour has linearly varying vortex strength γ on straight panels. Analytic line integrals produce the stream-function and velocity influence coefficients. The interior is held at rest: the stream function equals one internal constant at every contour node, so each panel has zero net normal flux. Pointwise normal velocity between nodes retains discretization error and is reported separately. γ₀ + γN = 0 at the sharp trailing edge makes equal speeds leave both surfaces, and zero interior velocity along the trailing-edge bisector, at half the shorter edge panel inside the edge, closes the doubled edge node. Pivoted LU gives the N+1 nodal strengths and the internal constant. With the interior at rest the surface speed is |γ|. Bernoulli gives Cp; pressure integration gives cl and cm¼ (Cp at each panel midpoint times its length), the exact integral of the quadratic panel Cp with its panel couples is also reported, and −2Γ/(Vc) provides a circulation cross-check with this clockwise-contour convention.</p>
+    <p><b>Read cl and CL as different quantities.</b> Lower-case <b>cl = L′/(q∞ c)</b> belongs to a 2D airfoil or a named wing strip; upper-case <b>CL = L/(q∞ S)</b> belongs to the finite wing. The validation table's “2D application cl” is the independent airfoil solver at its stated geometry and α. It is not the current wing's CL or a reference target supplied to the solver. Compare only matching geometry, α, model and reference area.</p>
+    <p><b>The magnified trailing-edge error is real discretization error.</b> Curves at matching physical x/c need not lie on the exact curve for a finite panel mesh. The last panels have different sample positions as N changes, so they are not identical points; the validation records their same-point errors separately. For the exact Joukowski cusp at 4°, the largest error among the last three panels falls from 0.0165 to 0.0100 to 0.00587 at 80/160/320 total panels. This is improving, not exact. The two exact cusp surfaces approach a common finite Cp at the edge; Kutta does not require their last finite-panel midpoint values to match or to be zero.</p>
     <p>Trailing edge: a finite edge angle is a stagnation point of exact potential flow only at the edge point itself, approached through a layer far thinner than any panel; a cusp (zero edge angle) has a finite, non-stagnant edge speed. The nodal value at the edge is a panel-scale value in both cases. The earlier version of this solver imposed no-penetration only at panel midpoints; at a cusp that let equal and opposite edge strengths (an interior jet) grow without bound and produced a last-panel Cp spike.</p>
     <p>The selected and half-density solutions are overlaid. Their difference is a sensitivity check, not a guaranteed error bound. Click a Cp point for its value and Vt/V∞; use Show panels to inspect contour nodes. File exports PNG and CSV. Any residual pressure drag is a discretization error, not physical profile drag. Unlike full XFOIL, this solver has no viscous boundary-layer coupling.</p>
     <p>Independent formulation references: <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://web.mit.edu/drela/Public/web/xfoil/xfoil_doc.txt">Drela &amp; Youngren: XFOIL formulation and viscous capabilities</a> (formulation reference only; no XFOIL code is used); Katz &amp; Plotkin, Low-Speed Aerodynamics, sec. 11.4; <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://github.com/usuaero/MachLine">MachLine (MIT licence)</a>: surface velocity from the sheet jump and the interior state, quadratic panel pressure and its moment, adapted to 2D panels.</p>`}));
